            <ArticleTitle>Mechanisms of Benefit with Aspirin Therapy in Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>735-747</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0889-8561(16)30056-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.iac.2016.06.011</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD) is a clinical syndrome characterized by severe persistent asthma, hyperplastic eosinophilic sinusitis with nasal polyps, and an intolerance to aspirin and other NSAIDs that preferentially inhibit COX-1. For more than 30 years, aspirin desensitization has proven to be of significant long-term benefit in carefully selected patients with AERD. Despite this, the exact mechanisms behind the therapeutic effects of aspirin desensitization remain poorly understood. In this article, we review the current understanding of the mechanisms of aspirin desensitization and discuss future areas of investigation.</AbstractText>
